\begin{abstract}
Does detailed balance improve financial state forecasts or their value under a fixed allocation rule? An expanding-window comparison evaluates IID, unrestricted three-state Markov and reversible Markov forecasts of next-session open-to-close return terciles. Thresholds use only historical observations. SPY is the primary asset; QQQ and IWM are related-asset robustness applications. Each contributes 2,766 forecasts over 2015--2025. The unrestricted chain has the lowest point-estimate log loss and Brier score for every asset, but a Romano--Wolf stepdown over the six score contrasts leaves only QQQ decisively distinguishable from IID; IWM sits at the 5\% boundary and SPY does not reach it. Reversible-minus-unrestricted log-loss intervals include zero for all three assets. Exposure remains between 47.08\% and 52.73\% and the rule trades a round trip each day; at five basis points per side all three allocations lose more than 64\% cumulatively. An exact daily-return decomposition separates fixed half exposure from the probability-driven adjustment; approximate unrestricted-minus-IID mean net improvements are only 0.043, 0.020 and 0.073 basis points per day. Decomposing the scoring loss, the SPY and QQQ gains come almost entirely from the middle-versus-outer tercile component, whereas IWM also gains on the conditional upper-versus-lower component that the allocation weights far more heavily. The fitted unrestricted chains carry a non-zero circulation at every evaluation origin, with period means between 0.8 and 1.4 percentage points of daily flow, which detailed balance sets to zero by construction; the score cost of that removal stays inside a zero-including interval. Under fixed cut points, rolling estimation windows and a common start date the comparisons keep their sign, while five of the fifteen asset-by-rule intervals no longer exclude zero. A controlled simulation reproduces the sign and order of magnitude of that cost at the observed training lengths, without identifying it as the cause of the ranking. The evidence establishes neither a forecast advantage from detailed balance nor profitability of this allocation at the primary cost. It does not establish equivalence of the forecast models or absence of economic value under other decision rules.
\end{abstract}

